Z||000000||2M4 Dale||05-18-2000||01:39 AM||||Anyone have suggestions on a good one? Although, I'm never very far from my car I would like to keep it and my modest sound system. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||05-18-2000||09:11 AM||||I've always had a current sensing type of alarm...had it on my past car and put it on my Fiero...it's 12 years old now and still works great. Very sensitive to shock and vibration and also very easy to install...just one wire to hook up.<P>Lots of people make them and they're reasonably priced.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||05-18-2000||10:33 AM||||I've installed Chapman's, Micro's, Alpine's,<BR>Clifford's, and Viper's. All the above systems work very well. The only thing I can say is buy the kit and install it yourself. It is alot cheaper than paying someone else. Most alarms have many options with them that all you need to do is hook up a wire or 2 and the alarm places charge $30 to $50 to "add" a feature! Also putting the brain box in a spot that is hard to get to. The reason is most alarm places install the brain box in the same place on same vehicles (usually right under the dash). If a theif wants a particular type of car he just has a place install the alarm and then figures out how to get past it.  Solder all connections, most alarm places use "qwik klips" and after a year or 2 the alarm starts to "act-up" due to bad connections. Crutchfield sells Crime Guard brand whichnis made by Clifford. They also give you very detailed drawings as to what wires to cut/splice into.||||reg||1
